(325528, 23300, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil (&amp; fellow Michiganian)<br/> <br/>Composition - Well I don''t really know what I''m looking at and your description is not helping me either.  Looks to me like Elmers glue and some slime.  That is relatively irrelevant what exactly the components are.  The glass on this one seems too centered and I don''t really like the perspective you chose to portray this.  I think shooting this from top of glass level or even underneath it a little would make it a bit more interesting.  A little splash of color in the whole pic is not really enough for me.  There seems to be little of interest things besides the green part.  The shadow going diagonally behind the glass is a bit distracting.  Something on the back of the glass is just about glowing whether its a glare or some of the funky white stuff.<br/> <br/>Technical - Looks pretty well taken.  maybe just a little underexposed but just a fuzz if any.  Focus is pretty good.  Really seems like there isn''t anything wrong with the way this picture is taken.  My feeling is, if you didn''t know what you were doing, you probably wouldn''t own a D100.<br/> <br/>Overall - Not a bad shot, but not very compelling.  A few distractions here and there.  It needs more colorful vibrant stuff.  Like that little bit of green does not breathe life into the whole picture.  It needs more than that methinks.  Well, good luck and keep shooting.  - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil (& fellow Michiganian) Composition - Well I don''t really know what I''m looking at and your description is not helping me either.  Looks to me like Elmers glue and some slime.  That is relatively irrelevant what exactly the components are.  The glass on this one seems too centered and I don''t really like the perspective you chose to portray this.  I think shooting this from top of glass level or even underneath it a little would make it a bit more interesting.  A little splash of color in the whole pic is not really enough for me.  There seems to be little of interest things besides the green part.  The shadow going diagonally behind the glass is a bit distracting.  Something on the back of the glass is just about glowing whether its a glare or some of the funky white stuff. Technical - Looks pretty well taken.  maybe just a little underexposed but just a fuzz if any.  Focus is pretty good.  Really seems like there isn''t anything wrong with the way this picture is taken.  My feeling is, if you didn''t know what you were doing, you probably wouldn''t own a D100. Overall - Not a bad shot, but not very compelling.  A few distractions here and there.  It needs more colorful vibrant stuff.  Like that little bit of green does not breathe life into the whole picture.  It needs more than that methinks.  Well, good luck and keep shooting.  - Inspzil', '2003-06-15 09:35:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(325534, 23770, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - You needed a little more of a plan for this one.  I''m not saying you couldn''t pull this off, but this one needs some help.  I''m not really sure what you were trying to show here.  I would say its the cheese judging by the title.  I think you should''ve shown more of that, but not from so close!  Give it some room to move a little.  The actual composition of the pic is pretty weak, but I want to look at the technical part of it first.  I may or may not come back to this section.<br/>(cont.)  The background of this picture is way way busy.  It should be simple and neat.  <br/> <br/>Technical - I think the biggest problem is the focus.  I think that stems from being too close to your subject.  The camera can''t focus if you are that close, or apparently that close because of the zoom.  Looks like the flash fired judging by the reflections on the spoon though I am not positive.  I would hesitate to use the flash, especially on something this close.  The colors do look okay, so that''s good.  <br/> <br/>Overall - aesthetically not a strong picture, although its making me crave nachos, and not very well taken.  I''d say turn off the flash from this close, back up a little, and try to take the photo from the direction where the background is minimized.  Your camera will take some very nice pics.  I bought my wife one in Oct. 2002.  Good luck to you and keep shooting. - Bob<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - You needed a little more of a plan for this one.  I''m not saying you couldn''t pull this off, but this one needs some help.  I''m not really sure what you were trying to show here.  I would say its the cheese judging by the title.  I think you should''ve shown more of that, but not from so close!  Give it some room to move a little.  The actual composition of the pic is pretty weak, but I want to look at the technical part of it first.  I may or may not come back to this section.(cont.)  The background of this picture is way way busy.  It should be simple and neat.   Technical - I think the biggest problem is the focus.  I think that stems from being too close to your subject.  The camera can''t focus if you are that close, or apparently that close because of the zoom.  Looks like the flash fired judging by the reflections on the spoon though I am not positive.  I would hesitate to use the flash, especially on something this close.  The colors do look okay, so that''s good.   Overall - aesthetically not a strong picture, although its making me crave nachos, and not very well taken.  I''d say turn off the flash from this close, back up a little, and try to take the photo from the direction where the background is minimized.  Your camera will take some very nice pics.  I bought my wife one in Oct. 2002.  Good luck to you and keep shooting. - Bob', '2003-06-15 09:49:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(325551, 23702, 9073, '<td valign="top">*critique club*<br/> <br/>Overall<br/>The rich colour palate certainly supports the feeling of a Sweeter Treat. You have succeeded in creating a warm atmosphere, a cosy image probably fitting a commercial use if the logo were present. Technically, your choice of aperture captures the detail well, your shutter speed stops the movement crisply, your lighting presents the material reasonably securely and your composition is cropped cleanly with no superfluous elements. There appears to be some blurring on the bottle cap, but otherwise, the shot has been executed well. You have captured the flavour (sorry, the pun had to be put in somewhere, best at the beginning) of the challenge by presenting not only liquid but a function of the liquid. To this extent, the shot succeeds. However, this success is limited when purely graphic elements are considered.<br/> <br/>Artistic and Conceptual Considerations<br/>I have a number of problems with this image. Graphically, a key element is the repeating arcs. However, due to the camera angle chosen and the choice of pitch black background, these arcs conflict rather than complement each other. I would have preferred a dual tone background, one which sets off the horizontal from the vertical. At present, the bowl seems to be at a warped angle to the background. A lower camera angle would have equalised the arcs and balanced the angle of the bowl to the background. In other words, there is a lack of perspective which makes viewing the image difficult, although the pitch black backing is classy. I can see why you would like to use it.<br/> <br/>Returning to the arc theme, a horizontal bottle cap would have continued the theme nicely, and you might have taken trouble to ensure that all arcs were of the same angle, or if different, pleasingly contrastive. The bottle needs to be lifted for gravity to make the liquid fall out. Could the lines of the bottle be positioned to balance those of the glass? <br/> <br/>I feel that the pitch black backing was a mistake, not only for the reasons mentioned above. To ensure the integrity of the black, you needed to use a fast shutter speed and some exposure compensation. You spot lit areas of the scene which emphasised graphic elements  the arcs  (I suspect unintentionally) in order to provide enough light for the actual thematic elements  the ice-cream, syrup and bottle. Better would have been to have provided a much more balanced lighting, avoiding the unwanted reflection on the left-hand side of the glass, chose a background colour to complement the colours of the elements and exposed for the syrup itself. At present, the syrup, ostensibly the main element, is hardly visible. I suggest that you consult a colour chart for complementary or contrastive colours for the syrup and place that at the back. Youll find the tonal range of the resulting image considerably different from the present one, but more vital and, possibly, more rewarding. <br/> <br/>The weakness of the syrup is made even more problematic by pouring it *behind* the ice-cream. At the very least, it should come directly on top, better still, to the front of the image. Compositionally, a lower camera angle would force a scene with a much higher level of ice-cream, above the bowl rim level (and of course, more to eat later!). Then, you could be afforded with a more face-on camera angle which would emphasise the space between the bottle cap and the ice-cream mountain. (A well-lit light-cream backing would really be effective here in turning the syrup stream into a viable graphic element.) I would also suggest you take a white balance reading from the ice-cream, which would help align the colour coordination throughout the scene, although I dont believe that there are any colour problems here with your presentation.<br/> <br/>A Second Overall<br/>You executed your vision well. However, that vision was not entirely inspired nor executed with a concept of graphic understanding. The shots that won this particular challenge were all completely wonderful in their use of the camera as a device that captures prepared graphics. This shot has some of that potential. I hope that you find my suggestions useful.<br/> <br/>Best wishes,<br/> <br/>Jim<br/></td>', '*critique club* OverallThe rich colour palate certainly supports the feeling of a Sweeter Treat. You have succeeded in creating a warm atmosphere, a cosy image probably fitting a commercial use if the logo were present. Technically, your choice of aperture captures the detail well, your shutter speed stops the movement crisply, your lighting presents the material reasonably securely and your composition is cropped cleanly with no superfluous elements. There appears to be some blurring on the bottle cap, but otherwise, the shot has been executed well. You have captured the flavour (sorry, the pun had to be put in somewhere, best at the beginning) of the challenge by presenting not only liquid but a function of the liquid. To this extent, the shot succeeds. However, this success is limited when purely graphic elements are considered. Artistic and Conceptual ConsiderationsI have a number of problems with this image. Graphically, a key element is the repeating arcs. However, due to the camera angle chosen and the choice of pitch black background, these arcs conflict rather than complement each other. I would have preferred a dual tone background, one which sets off the horizontal from the vertical. At present, the bowl seems to be at a warped angle to the background. A lower camera angle would have equalised the arcs and balanced the angle of the bowl to the background. In other words, there is a lack of perspective which makes viewing the image difficult, although the pitch black backing is classy. I can see why you would like to use it. Returning to the arc theme, a horizontal bottle cap would have continued the theme nicely, and you might have taken trouble to ensure that all arcs were of the same angle, or if different, pleasingly contrastive. The bottle needs to be lifted for gravity to make the liquid fall out. Could the lines of the bottle be positioned to balance those of the glass?  I feel that the pitch black backing was a mistake, not only for the reasons mentioned above. To ensure the integrity of the black, you needed to use a fast shutter speed and some exposure compensation. You spot lit areas of the scene which emphasised graphic elements  the arcs  (I suspect unintentionally) in order to provide enough light for the actual thematic elements  the ice-cream, syrup and bottle. Better would have been to have provided a much more balanced lighting, avoiding the unwanted reflection on the left-hand side of the glass, chose a background colour to complement the colours of the elements and exposed for the syrup itself. At present, the syrup, ostensibly the main element, is hardly visible. I suggest that you consult a colour chart for complementary or contrastive colours for the syrup and place that at the back. Youll find the tonal range of the resulting image considerably different from the present one, but more vital and, possibly, more rewarding.  The weakness of the syrup is made even more problematic by pouring it *behind* the ice-cream. At the very least, it should come directly on top, better still, to the front of the image. Compositionally, a lower camera angle would force a scene with a much higher level of ice-cream, above the bowl rim level (and of course, more to eat later!). Then, you could be afforded with a more face-on camera angle which would emphasise the space between the bottle cap and the ice-cream mountain. (A well-lit light-cream backing would really be effective here in turning the syrup stream into a viable graphic element.) I would also suggest you take a white balance reading from the ice-cream, which would help align the colour coordination throughout the scene, although I dont believe that there are any colour problems here with your presentation. A Second OverallYou executed your vision well. However, that vision was not entirely inspired nor executed with a concept of graphic understanding. The shots that won this particular challenge were all completely wonderful in their use of the camera as a device that captures prepared graphics. This shot has some of that potential. I hope that you find my suggestions useful. Best wishes, Jim', '2003-06-15 10:35:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(325552, 23702, 9073, '<td valign="top">Ignore this posting. I can''t seem to get back to my home screen without typing something in here.</td>', 'Ignore this posting. I can''t seem to get back to my home screen without typing something in here.', '2003-06-15 10:38:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(325575, 23761, 9073, '<td valign="top">*critique club*<br/> <br/>Preamble. <br/>Dont look at this as a photograph and think What is it?. That kind of thinking reduces photography to just a guessing game, a quiz. Rather, imagine that this was painted. How would your reaction be then? <br/> <br/>Overall<br/>The first impression one gets on viewing this image is the need to work out what the image actually is  an enigma, so to speak. The challenge (not in the DPC sense) therefore, is to create an image that is worthy of such investment of time and mental energy. Beyond that, if the conclusion (if such a word can be used) reveals something wonderful or vital or valuable, so much the better. <br/> <br/>The only clues I had were the title and the image itself. Rather quickly, given that this is a liquid challenge, I realised that you had used some kind of oil as your base (forgive the pun). Now, I had to gauge whether or not the choice was appropriate. Certainly, there is a feeling of texture here. Your palate is lavish and your brush strokes fluent. There is intricacy, but not so much as would be inappropriate in a real oil painting. The focus is . . . <br/> <br/>I stopped that sentence midway because, for the first time, I had realised that I was talking about a photograph, not an oil painting. My language until that point, as well as my projected thinking, was relevant to traditional art. So, in that real sense, with me, youve succeeded to a remarkable degree, at that level.<br/> <br/>Artistic and Conceptual considerations<br/>Your tonal palate is very well enhanced by the sense of light pervading the image. It is as if you took a single colour line and used every hue on it  the lights, the darks, the in-betweens, they are all beautiful and superbly balanced. Ive a little quibble over the sharpness in the bottom left, however. <br/> <br/>Coming to the lines themselves, the patterns are clear enough, and the contrasts effective. I particularly like the bubbles between the rising arcs. The strong line at the top 3/4 mark serves as a powerful balance to the more luxurious expanse in the bottom right. Indeed, the whole picture seems to show a movement of some contraption moving left-wards leaving a trail in the right. The space you left on the right really helps this feeling, as do the bubbles in the track left by the object. Its a pity that the focus on the left wasnt as clear as the right. If so, the sense would be complete. <br/> <br/>Theres a problem with that. The focal plane must have been the same, meaning that the focus must be the same throughout. So, why is the left-hand side fuzzy? Its not. Just that there are far more bits. The right-hand side is less busy, so details can stand being out-of-focus. The answer is that the whole shot is out-of-focus, only that the left shows it more. However, the focus is not really a problem, and it certainly doesnt detract from the overall shot.<br/> <br/>[time out]<br/>Ive just had a conversation about this photograph with my wife, who has a good sense for a non-photographer. Without having read the comments left during the challenge, she mimicked those who had tried to guess what the object was. I suppose that thats one of the challenges art photographers have  people know that their subject existed in space and therefore they try to guess what it is. For me, the only difference between photographers and artists is that, whereas artists can fabricate their subject entirely from their minds, we photographers have to look around us to find subjects capable of artistic treatment. Rather than be amazed as the beauty that is all around us in tiny aspects, I would like others to view this kind of photography as created art as it is. Then judge it as they would any other art work. <br/> <br/>I dont know about art, but I know whats I like (sic) is a perfectly valid response to art (even though it does allow for moronic opinions). Thats the ultimate truth. One commenter (Autool  a very respected member of this community) said that he wouldnt want to hang it. Thats the rub. If we choose this kind of subject, we must be open to that level of criticism.<br/> <br/>For me? I wouldnt hang it, either. But I would, and will, look at it again and again. For one, the sheer imagination of seeing such dynamics in a simple oil bottle is a reminder to us all that our subjects are all around us. For two, there is real dynamics in the perceived movement of the object through the painting. And, for three, any object which inspires real thought should be remembered. Thank you.<br/> <br/>Unlike my normal practice in critiques, Ive given no suggestions for improvement because art works (well executed like this one) must be judged aesthetically. <br/> <br/>Best wishes,<br/> <br/>Jim<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/></td>', '*critique club* Preamble. Dont look at this as a photograph and think What is it?. That kind of thinking reduces photography to just a guessing game, a quiz. Rather, imagine that this was painted. How would your reaction be then?  OverallThe first impression one gets on viewing this image is the need to work out what the image actually is  an enigma, so to speak. The challenge (not in the DPC sense) therefore, is to create an image that is worthy of such investment of time and mental energy. Beyond that, if the conclusion (if such a word can be used) reveals something wonderful or vital or valuable, so much the better.  The only clues I had were the title and the image itself. Rather quickly, given that this is a liquid challenge, I realised that you had used some kind of oil as your base (forgive the pun). Now, I had to gauge whether or not the choice was appropriate. Certainly, there is a feeling of texture here. Your palate is lavish and your brush strokes fluent. There is intricacy, but not so much as would be inappropriate in a real oil painting. The focus is . . .  I stopped that sentence midway because, for the first time, I had realised that I was talking about a photograph, not an oil painting. My language until that point, as well as my projected thinking, was relevant to traditional art. So, in that real sense, with me, youve succeeded to a remarkable degree, at that level. Artistic and Conceptual considerationsYour tonal palate is very well enhanced by the sense of light pervading the image. It is as if you took a single colour line and used every hue on it  the lights, the darks, the in-betweens, they are all beautiful and superbly balanced. Ive a little quibble over the sharpness in the bottom left, however.  Coming to the lines themselves, the patterns are clear enough, and the contrasts effective. I particularly like the bubbles between the rising arcs. The strong line at the top 3/4 mark serves as a powerful balance to the more luxurious expanse in the bottom right. Indeed, the whole picture seems to show a movement of some contraption moving left-wards leaving a trail in the right. The space you left on the right really helps this feeling, as do the bubbles in the track left by the object. Its a pity that the focus on the left wasnt as clear as the right. If so, the sense would be complete.  Theres a problem with that. The focal plane must have been the same, meaning that the focus must be the same throughout. So, why is the left-hand side fuzzy? Its not. Just that there are far more bits. The right-hand side is less busy, so details can stand being out-of-focus. The answer is that the whole shot is out-of-focus, only that the left shows it more. However, the focus is not really a problem, and it certainly doesnt detract from the overall shot. [time out]Ive just had a conversation about this photograph with my wife, who has a good sense for a non-photographer. Without having read the comments left during the challenge, she mimicked those who had tried to guess what the object was. I suppose that thats one of the challenges art photographers have  people know that their subject existed in space and therefore they try to guess what it is. For me, the only difference between photographers and artists is that, whereas artists can fabricate their subject entirely from their minds, we photographers have to look around us to find subjects capable of artistic treatment. Rather than be amazed as the beauty that is all around us in tiny aspects, I would like others to view this kind of photography as created art as it is. Then judge it as they would any other art work.  I dont know about art, but I know whats I like (sic) is a perfectly valid response to art (even though it does allow for moronic opinions). Thats the ultimate truth. One commenter (Autool  a very respected member of this community) said that he wouldnt want to hang it. Thats the rub. If we choose this kind of subject, we must be open to that level of criticism. For me? I wouldnt hang it, either. But I would, and will, look at it again and again. For one, the sheer imagination of seeing such dynamics in a simple oil bottle is a reminder to us all that our subjects are all around us. For two, there is real dynamics in the perceived movement of the object through the painting. And, for three, any object which inspires real thought should be remembered. Thank you. Unlike my normal practice in critiques, Ive given no suggestions for improvement because art works (well executed like this one) must be judged aesthetically.  Best wishes, Jim    ', '2003-06-15 11:36:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(325576, 23506, 9073, '<td valign="top">*critique club*<br/> <br/>Writing personally, one of the reasons why I became a member of DPC was to develop my technique as a single-shot photographer. I was heavily involved with the more traditional arts in the past, but now, I am pursing an interest in photography as a modern art. <br/> <br/>Why am I writing this rather than critique this photograph? Having studied this shot for some time, seeing that both faces are identical  needing a timed exposure and a model who can produce the exact position, different light casts on different parts of the canvass  requiring a specially-made filter, seeing two vertical lines on the left-hand side which interrupt the flow  the ready-made filter again?, and not being given any information about the making of this shot, I can be forgiven to believing that this is not a single shot, but a computer-aided graphic. Now, theres no inherent problem with that, only on this site, we only accept single-shot entries. If it is, Id really love to see how you did it. When you do, Ill write a full critique, I promise.<br/> <br/>By the way, I actually like the image. The word purple has strong connotations of contemplation, worry and despair, all of which are present here.<br/></td>', '*critique club* Writing personally, one of the reasons why I became a member of DPC was to develop my technique as a single-shot photographer. I was heavily involved with the more traditional arts in the past, but now, I am pursing an interest in photography as a modern art.  Why am I writing this rather than critique this photograph? Having studied this shot for some time, seeing that both faces are identical  needing a timed exposure and a model who can produce the exact position, different light casts on different parts of the canvass  requiring a specially-made filter, seeing two vertical lines on the left-hand side which interrupt the flow  the ready-made filter again?, and not being given any information about the making of this shot, I can be forgiven to believing that this is not a single shot, but a computer-aided graphic. Now, theres no inherent problem with that, only on this site, we only accept single-shot entries. If it is, Id really love to see how you did it. When you do, Ill write a full critique, I promise. By the way, I actually like the image. The word purple has strong connotations of contemplation, worry and despair, all of which are present here.', '2003-06-15 12:03:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(325725, 23385, 2000, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>FIRST IMPRESSION: Looks like a frog.  I like the contrast with the solid black background, and the light reflections on the gloop.<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE: It''s liquid, and it''s not water, so the challenge is met. <br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: The framing, with the leading edge of the liquid not touching the side, gives a feeling of movement.  Again, the lighting here is very well done, with good reflections and no real hot spots. <br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: I''m assuming macro was used here, and that''s most likely a good part of why it came out with the level of gloop detail.  However, the edges are a bit fuzzier than I''d like to see them, especially visible in the front of the gloop. <br/> <br/>CONCLUSION: A good interpretation of the challenge, well shot and executed.  The depth of field/focus issue may be difficult to address in macro photography, if that''s the mode used for this shot, but the detail and lighting make this a very nice shot. <br/> <br/>Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!</td>', '*Critique Club*  FIRST IMPRESSION: Looks like a frog.  I like the contrast with the solid black background, and the light reflections on the gloop. CHALLENGE: It''s liquid, and it''s not water, so the challenge is met.  COMPOSITION: The framing, with the leading edge of the liquid not touching the side, gives a feeling of movement.  Again, the lighting here is very well done, with good reflections and no real hot spots.  TECHNICAL: I''m assuming macro was used here, and that''s most likely a good part of why it came out with the level of gloop detail.  However, the edges are a bit fuzzier than I''d like to see them, especially visible in the front of the gloop.  CONCLUSION: A good interpretation of the challenge, well shot and executed.  The depth of field/focus issue may be difficult to address in macro photography, if that''s the mode used for this shot, but the detail and lighting make this a very nice shot.  Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!', '2003-06-15 17:40:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(325729, 23689, 2000, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>FIRST IMPRESSION: Maple syrup... but seeing as it''s Karo Syrup, I wasn''t too far off.  The specks on the spoon are a bit too pronounced, I think.<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE: "Liquid, not water" was the challenge - as you''ve used karo syrup, the challenge is met.<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: There''s something about that spoon that I''m not crazy about, maybe its darkness, or those glare spots.  I like the liquidity of this shot, with the immersion effect of the poured syrup into the pool.<br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: A bit of glare, and a little bit in the way of too much contrast between the spoon and the shadows.  Since the bowl is not really an integral part of the shot, its defocused edges work well to bring the eyes back to the syrup in the bowl. <br/> <br/>CONCLUSION: Meets the challenge well, and holds my interest as a viewer.  The glare specks in the spoon, and the color/flatness of the spoon itself are a slight problem, but overall it''s a good piece of work. <br/> <br/>Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!</td>', '*Critique Club*  FIRST IMPRESSION: Maple syrup... but seeing as it''s Karo Syrup, I wasn''t too far off.  The specks on the spoon are a bit too pronounced, I think. CHALLENGE: "Liquid, not water" was the challenge - as you''ve used karo syrup, the challenge is met. COMPOSITION: There''s something about that spoon that I''m not crazy about, maybe its darkness, or those glare spots.  I like the liquidity of this shot, with the immersion effect of the poured syrup into the pool. TECHNICAL: A bit of glare, and a little bit in the way of too much contrast between the spoon and the shadows.  Since the bowl is not really an integral part of the shot, its defocused edges work well to bring the eyes back to the syrup in the bowl.  CONCLUSION: Meets the challenge well, and holds my interest as a viewer.  The glare specks in the spoon, and the color/flatness of the spoon itself are a slight problem, but overall it''s a good piece of work.  Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!', '2003-06-15 17:45:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(325777, 23297, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>This definately says liquid, can''t argue that.  Its very well composed and appears to be well thought out.  Your only problem with the entire image is the darkness of the cherries, they are really just to dark for the rest of the image.  Other than that the photo is really good.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* This definately says liquid, can''t argue that.  Its very well composed and appears to be well thought out.  Your only problem with the entire image is the darkness of the cherries, they are really just to dark for the rest of the image.  Other than that the photo is really good. Anna', '2003-06-15 19:49:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(325833, 23457, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>This definately fits the challenge.  Your focus is good but you may want to try a shallower dof to blurr the background somewhat.  As I look at it the silver colander in the background is very distracting.  <br/> <br/>The container of syrup is just a little to dark on the left side and could use a tad more light.  In all it is a very good take on the liquid challenge and with just a few minor corrections would be outstanding.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* This definately fits the challenge.  Your focus is good but you may want to try a shallower dof to blurr the background somewhat.  As I look at it the silver colander in the background is very distracting.   The container of syrup is just a little to dark on the left side and could use a tad more light.  In all it is a very good take on the liquid challenge and with just a few minor corrections would be outstanding. Anna', '2003-06-15 22:30:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(325918, 23806, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>My first impression is that this is not appealing at all.  Not pleasant to look at, and makes me wrinkle up my face with discust.<br/>However, technically, this shot is great.<br/>The focus and clarity are unfortunately perfect.  Great detail in everything.  The kids eyes are stunning.<br/>The closeness is great.  And actually, I Tried this with a crop right above his upper lip, and I think that works even more.  His eyes are really powerful, and you could use that to your advantage here.  I''d like to see this with just the eyes and nose.  And snot of course, or it wouldn''t meet the challenge. lol<br/>I think that the lighting is just fine.  Not to bright, not too dark.  If I HAD to make a suggestion, I''d suggest trying a little more lighting, but totally not absolutely needed.<br/>Overall technically well done, just not something I''d like to look at for a long time.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*My first impression is that this is not appealing at all.  Not pleasant to look at, and makes me wrinkle up my face with discust.However, technically, this shot is great.The focus and clarity are unfortunately perfect.  Great detail in everything.  The kids eyes are stunning.The closeness is great.  And actually, I Tried this with a crop right above his upper lip, and I think that works even more.  His eyes are really powerful, and you could use that to your advantage here.  I''d like to see this with just the eyes and nose.  And snot of course, or it wouldn''t meet the challenge. lolI think that the lighting is just fine.  Not to bright, not too dark.  If I HAD to make a suggestion, I''d suggest trying a little more lighting, but totally not absolutely needed.Overall technically well done, just not something I''d like to look at for a long time.~Heather~', '2003-06-16 00:01:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(326400, 23701, 1863, '<td valign="top">Hi snowleopard10101 <br/> <br/>This is your critique for Sealing Liquid, taken for the Liquid challenge.<br/> <br/>Let me get this out of the way, it fits the challenge in my eyes!<br/> <br/>Moving on to the composition: like frisca said, I like the way the clutter seems to indicate an assembly line at a factory. I think presentation is everything though, and since you didn''t give me any Photographer''s Comments to work on... I''ll make assumptions :) I would have prefered to see all the ribbons facing forward for a bit more of a symmetrical feel to the photograph. I would also have liked to see more of the tops of the bottles, to get the "complete" feel that this seems to lack.<br/> <br/>While I like the photograph, I think that flash photography on something like glass can easily go wrong. As you can see there''s a strange glare from the flash going on there. I think you need some kind of diffuser... Have you tried to perhaps put up different light with a piece of paper to bounce the harshness away? I wish I could help more in this aspect, but you didn''t give me more to work on.<br/> <br/>Overall, I like the idea of this photograph, but I think you can do so much more :) I can even imagine it. Thank you for submitting it, and good luck on upcoming challenges!<br/>-Annida</td>', 'Hi snowleopard10101  This is your critique for Sealing Liquid, taken for the Liquid challenge. Let me get this out of the way, it fits the challenge in my eyes! Moving on to the composition: like frisca said, I like the way the clutter seems to indicate an assembly line at a factory. I think presentation is everything though, and since you didn''t give me any Photographer''s Comments to work on... I''ll make assumptions :) I would have prefered to see all the ribbons facing forward for a bit more of a symmetrical feel to the photograph. I would also have liked to see more of the tops of the bottles, to get the "complete" feel that this seems to lack. While I like the photograph, I think that flash photography on something like glass can easily go wrong. As you can see there''s a strange glare from the flash going on there. I think you need some kind of diffuser... Have you tried to perhaps put up different light with a piece of paper to bounce the harshness away? I wish I could help more in this aspect, but you didn''t give me more to work on. Overall, I like the idea of this photograph, but I think you can do so much more :) I can even imagine it. Thank you for submitting it, and good luck on upcoming challenges!-Annida', '2003-06-16 10:41:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(326432, 23700, 6900, '<td valign="top">izraziti zadovoljistvo! voz lijep slika.<br/>(congratulations!  It is a beautiful picture)</td>', 'izraziti zadovoljistvo! voz lijep slika.(congratulations!  It is a beautiful picture)', '2003-06-16 11:11:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(326630, 23651, 7758, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Hi Johnothan,<br/> <br/>intersting image and choice of cropping. It is certainly abstract and I think it probably scored lower than it should have. I like the design.<br/>One thing that does detract from the image is the couple of little white spots on the inner two swirls. I take it that the big white spot is the reflection of the lamp you used to light the picture. I think it both adds and detracts from the image. Firstly I think it adds something to the surface effect giving a sense of the irregularities in the surface, though at the same time it is distracting and probably cost you a few votes. It seems fairly grainy which may have been the effect you were going for. Personally I think I would have perhaps used a longer exposure to lift the image a bit and moved the lighting source out of the direct line of the shot.<br/>Perhaps a longer exposure at a lower ISO would have reduced the grain. Finally increase the saturation to make the colours a bit more vivid.<br/>Compositionally, I think it is a good abstract and thew swirls do lead your eye to the centre of the image.<br/>I think it would be worth reshooting this to see what else you could get out of it.<br/>Overall I think a few technical and compositional changes would have resulted in better scores for this image.<br/> <br/>Thanks for sharing your image and keep shooting.<br/> <br/>Cheers,<br/> <br/>Todd.</td>', 'Critique Club Hi Johnothan, intersting image and choice of cropping. It is certainly abstract and I think it probably scored lower than it should have. I like the design.One thing that does detract from the image is the couple of little white spots on the inner two swirls. I take it that the big white spot is the reflection of the lamp you used to light the picture. I think it both adds and detracts from the image. Firstly I think it adds something to the surface effect giving a sense of the irregularities in the surface, though at the same time it is distracting and probably cost you a few votes. It seems fairly grainy which may have been the effect you were going for. Personally I think I would have perhaps used a longer exposure to lift the image a bit and moved the lighting source out of the direct line of the shot.Perhaps a longer exposure at a lower ISO would have reduced the grain. Finally increase the saturation to make the colours a bit more vivid.Compositionally, I think it is a good abstract and thew swirls do lead your eye to the centre of the image.I think it would be worth reshooting this to see what else you could get out of it.Overall I think a few technical and compositional changes would have resulted in better scores for this image. Thanks for sharing your image and keep shooting. Cheers, Todd.', '2003-06-16 13:43:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(326652, 23474, 5112, '<td valign="top">FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Hi, Little Bunny,<br/> <br/>Guess who got your picture to critique for the Critique Club?  ME!!!<br/> <br/>OK.  I think this was a good entry, especially for your first time entering a picture to a challenge.  I thought it was really nice how you came up with the idea all by yourself, and set it up and took it all by yourself.  I think you''re going to be a good photographer when you grow up.<br/> <br/>Like a couple persons said below, it would have been nice that the top candle were a different colour or colours (it DOES look a little bit like "you know what").  Also, it might have been better if you would have used some other form of light rather than the flash (that would have helped a lot with both reflections on the metal and shadows).  I think that you could also have turned the candleholder so that the label doesn''t show.  <br/> <br/>Things that I like are the black background, the way you got in quite close to the candles to take the picture, and that it is nicely in focus.<br/> <br/>I think, little bunny, that one of the problems with taking pictures is that what looks interesting to me (or you) may not look all that interesting to other people.  So, your problem as a photographer is not only to take a good picture, but one that will get people to stop and take another look, and wonder about it, and come back, both for the beauty of it and the story behind it.<br/> <br/>Keep going, sweetie, but remember, the Fuji is still mine for a little while longer :)  I love you,<br/> <br/>Mami (uabresch)<br/></td>', 'FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB Hi, Little Bunny, Guess who got your picture to critique for the Critique Club?  ME!!! OK.  I think this was a good entry, especially for your first time entering a picture to a challenge.  I thought it was really nice how you came up with the idea all by yourself, and set it up and took it all by yourself.  I think you''re going to be a good photographer when you grow up. Like a couple persons said below, it would have been nice that the top candle were a different colour or colours (it DOES look a little bit like "you know what").  Also, it might have been better if you would have used some other form of light rather than the flash (that would have helped a lot with both reflections on the metal and shadows).  I think that you could also have turned the candleholder so that the label doesn''t show.   Things that I like are the black background, the way you got in quite close to the candles to take the picture, and that it is nicely in focus. I think, little bunny, that one of the problems with taking pictures is that what looks interesting to me (or you) may not look all that interesting to other people.  So, your problem as a photographer is not only to take a good picture, but one that will get people to stop and take another look, and wonder about it, and come back, both for the beauty of it and the story behind it. Keep going, sweetie, but remember, the Fuji is still mine for a little while longer :)  I love you, Mami (uabresch)', '2003-06-16 13:58:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(326682, 23454, 5112, '<td valign="top">FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Hello, Bob,<br/> <br/>The lovely lady is really lovely!  So is this image.<br/> <br/>First impression:  This is one of those images that make you smile and think of words such as "magical".  The unexpected choice of BW works well here.  I say "unexpected" because at least in my mind bubbles are ussually associated with colour.  IMHO it meets the challenge just fine.<br/> <br/>Light:  Excellent.  The skin-tones are beautiful.  The light bubbles against the dark background are very good.  I''m wondering, how did you convert this picture to BW?  It must not be a straight conversion to greyscale.<br/> <br/>Focus:  Very good.  The face/hand are nicely in focus, the background is appropriately blurred, the bubbles are in and out, floating.  I really like how the bubble that''s being formed and the bubble wand show movement over time, and that even though the shutter speed you used wasn''t all that slow.<br/> <br/>Composition:  First, I love the square format for this shot.  The thin white mat is very appropriate.  Composition overall is excellent, with the exception of the path in the background.  It is hard to tell, but the picture might be better if the path were not there (so you have a lot of landscaping to do now!).<br/> <br/>Overall:  Very good entry to this challenge, and your final score shows it.  BTW, about your other picture in your portfolio, "Enjoying a Kiss", that is your wife again, right?  I hope that''s you with her!<br/> <br/>Take care,<br/> <br/>Ursula (uabresch)<br/>Comments, questions, complaints (oh no!) ... feel free to contact me.</td>', 'FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB Hello, Bob, The lovely lady is really lovely!  So is this image. First impression:  This is one of those images that make you smile and think of words such as "magical".  The unexpected choice of BW works well here.  I say "unexpected" because at least in my mind bubbles are ussually associated with colour.  IMHO it meets the challenge just fine. Light:  Excellent.  The skin-tones are beautiful.  The light bubbles against the dark background are very good.  I''m wondering, how did you convert this picture to BW?  It must not be a straight conversion to greyscale. Focus:  Very good.  The face/hand are nicely in focus, the background is appropriately blurred, the bubbles are in and out, floating.  I really like how the bubble that''s being formed and the bubble wand show movement over time, and that even though the shutter speed you used wasn''t all that slow. Composition:  First, I love the square format for this shot.  The thin white mat is very appropriate.  Composition overall is excellent, with the exception of the path in the background.  It is hard to tell, but the picture might be better if the path were not there (so you have a lot of landscaping to do now!). Overall:  Very good entry to this challenge, and your final score shows it.  BTW, about your other picture in your portfolio, "Enjoying a Kiss", that is your wife again, right?  I hope that''s you with her! Take care, Ursula (uabresch)Comments, questions, complaints (oh no!) ... feel free to contact me.', '2003-06-16 14:11:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(326848, 23442, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>I''m not really sure what I think about this.  I think that the little pieces of confetti floating around (or stuck in it, either way) are very neat.<br/>It''s all about the colors and shapes though.  It doesn''t LOOK liquid I guess is what I''m saying.  Had not been for the bubbles in the top of the cup, it might have been very difficult to tell.<br/>The colors are nice, and focus and clarity are right on.  Nice sharp lines in the heart, and lots of clear confetti.<br/>The smudged area on the red side near where it meets the yellow is a bit distracting.  I don''t know if I like that part at all.<br/>The crop does seem a little strange too.  I wonder if this should either be all in the frame, or more out of the frame.  one extremem or the other.<br/>Overall, it''s a calming photo to look at.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*I''m not really sure what I think about this.  I think that the little pieces of confetti floating around (or stuck in it, either way) are very neat.It''s all about the colors and shapes though.  It doesn''t LOOK liquid I guess is what I''m saying.  Had not been for the bubbles in the top of the cup, it might have been very difficult to tell.The colors are nice, and focus and clarity are right on.  Nice sharp lines in the heart, and lots of clear confetti.The smudged area on the red side near where it meets the yellow is a bit distracting.  I don''t know if I like that part at all.The crop does seem a little strange too.  I wonder if this should either be all in the frame, or more out of the frame.  one extremem or the other.Overall, it''s a calming photo to look at.~Heather~', '2003-06-16 15:41:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(326856, 23808, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC!<br/> <br/>COLOR &amp; FOCUS: The fluorenscent blue works very well with the black bg. Good choice! I don''t see any probs with your focus. <br/> <br/>LIGHTING: nothing to say. For this kind of shot I think you acheived what you wanted to acheived lighting wise. I wouldn''t change a thing. :)<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: ok.... good job on centering the glass.<br/> <br/>No I''m not sure whetherthe blue on the left side of the glass is a reflection or liquid. It looks more like liquid to me. Whatever it s it''s very distracting and breaks the "evenness" of the centered subject. There are lso some black spots on the ice in the middle of the glass. It looks like dust or something. It also revoves from the perfection of the shot. And what''s this!! The left border is thiker than the other side??  OOPS!!  :)  Gotta watch out for that!  ;-)<br/> <br/>All in all a good shot. I gave you 7 cuz of the originality and nice colors. The biggest challenge is getting the WOW! factor in and that''ll bring your photo in the 6s or higher!! There''s no secret recipe for that cuz I''m pretty inconsistant myself! :)<br/> <br/>Hitting a 5.2 on a 1st submission is good! Keep it up!<br/> <br/>Yanik</td>', 'Greetings from the CC! COLOR & FOCUS: The fluorenscent blue works very well with the black bg. Good choice! I don''t see any probs with your focus.  LIGHTING: nothing to say. For this kind of shot I think you acheived what you wanted to acheived lighting wise. I wouldn''t change a thing. :) COMPOSITION: ok.... good job on centering the glass. No I''m not sure whetherthe blue on the left side of the glass is a reflection or liquid. It looks more like liquid to me. Whatever it s it''s very distracting and breaks the "evenness" of the centered subject. There are lso some black spots on the ice in the middle of the glass. It looks like dust or something. It also revoves from the perfection of the shot. And what''s this!! The left border is thiker than the other side??  OOPS!!  :)  Gotta watch out for that!  ;-) All in all a good shot. I gave you 7 cuz of the originality and nice colors. The biggest challenge is getting the WOW! factor in and that''ll bring your photo in the 6s or higher!! There''s no secret recipe for that cuz I''m pretty inconsistant myself! :) Hitting a 5.2 on a 1st submission is good! Keep it up! Yanik', '2003-06-16 15:47:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(326871, 23777, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>This is where some kind of ''photographers comments'' are very helpful.  To me, this looks like one of those solid glass balls with bubbles in it.  I''m not really sure at all what this is.<br/>Yes, that does look like santa caluse in the bubble to the right.<br/>Focus seems a bit soft here, and I''m not really finding a particular main spot of interest.<br/>The really bright light reflection thing to the right is a distracting.<br/>Your colors are really nice though.  I think it''s a bright vivid red, and is very pretty red.<br/>Overall though, it doesn''t really make me jump out of my seat with excitement.  Maybe if we could tell what this was, or if the focus were better.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*This is where some kind of ''photographers comments'' are very helpful.  To me, this looks like one of those solid glass balls with bubbles in it.  I''m not really sure at all what this is.Yes, that does look like santa caluse in the bubble to the right.Focus seems a bit soft here, and I''m not really finding a particular main spot of interest.The really bright light reflection thing to the right is a distracting.Your colors are really nice though.  I think it''s a bright vivid red, and is very pretty red.Overall though, it doesn''t really make me jump out of my seat with excitement.  Maybe if we could tell what this was, or if the focus were better.~Heather~', '2003-06-16 16:04:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(327323, 23629, 1647, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition: I love the various textures included in this shot - the smooth mud, sharp rocks, rust coloured earth and even some steam.  It''s perhaps a few too many elements to combine but I actually kind of like it.  I have to admit though that the photo has two focal points - the mud and the pit.  A different crop may have made one focal point more powerful.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality: You have captured the colours and textures incredibly well.  I have nothing to complain about - no blown highlights, not oversharpened - just plain well done.<br/> <br/>Meeting Challenge: I guess some people might argue that mud is water, but I think you easily met the challenge.  It''s certainly an interesting form of liquid - especially its behaviour.<br/> <br/>Border / Title: The title is fine, and helps clarify what might otherwise me a confusing subject (the bubbling effect is laregly lost in a still photo).  I think this shot might have been framed better with a small border - and that''s probably the first time I have said a border would help.<br/> <br/>Overall: It''s a lovely shot, and will act as a great momento of your trip.  I really like the various textures - the smoothness is such a contrast to the sharpness of the surrounding rocks.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club Composition: I love the various textures included in this shot - the smooth mud, sharp rocks, rust coloured earth and even some steam.  It''s perhaps a few too many elements to combine but I actually kind of like it.  I have to admit though that the photo has two focal points - the mud and the pit.  A different crop may have made one focal point more powerful. Technical Quality: You have captured the colours and textures incredibly well.  I have nothing to complain about - no blown highlights, not oversharpened - just plain well done. Meeting Challenge: I guess some people might argue that mud is water, but I think you easily met the challenge.  It''s certainly an interesting form of liquid - especially its behaviour. Border / Title: The title is fine, and helps clarify what might otherwise me a confusing subject (the bubbling effect is laregly lost in a still photo).  I think this shot might have been framed better with a small border - and that''s probably the first time I have said a border would help. Overall: It''s a lovely shot, and will act as a great momento of your trip.  I really like the various textures - the smoothness is such a contrast to the sharpness of the surrounding rocks.', '2003-06-16 23:43:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(327623, 23723, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/> <br/>Very interesting image.  It appears to meet the challege as a liquid.  My question is what kind of liquid is it?  The bubbles in the liquid add interest to this.  It does appear to have been slightly over sharpened and possibly generally overprocessed.  <br/> <br/>Take your time when taking an image and make sure it is in focus, it will save you a lot of time editing later and will keep from having the over processed look.<br/> <br/>Anna<br/></td>', '*Critique Club*  Very interesting image.  It appears to meet the challege as a liquid.  My question is what kind of liquid is it?  The bubbles in the liquid add interest to this.  It does appear to have been slightly over sharpened and possibly generally overprocessed.   Take your time when taking an image and make sure it is in focus, it will save you a lot of time editing later and will keep from having the over processed look. Anna', '2003-06-17 10:19:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(327768, 23762, 3087, '<td valign="top"><b>Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts</b> <br/>Great colours and interesting centred composition.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content</b> <br/>I rather like this  I love the colour palette of blues and whites  very refreshing  reminds me of a minty toothpaste advert.<br/>Bubbles very clear  good lighting. Great texture.<br/> <br/> <b>Camera Work - Technical</b> <br/>Well lit, well exposed.<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing - Technical</b> <br/>Good crop. <br/> <br/><b>Fits The Challenge</b> <br/>Yes  its nice to see the qualities of a gel rather than a runny liquid. Though the use of the word jelly through me as in UK thats what we call jello.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo</b> <br/>Like it!<br/></td>', 'Critique Club   Initial thoughts Great colours and interesting centred composition. Composition/ Content I rather like this  I love the colour palette of blues and whites  very refreshing  reminds me of a minty toothpaste advert.Bubbles very clear  good lighting. Great texture.  Camera Work - Technical Well lit, well exposed. Digital Processing - Technical Good crop.  Fits The Challenge Yes  its nice to see the qualities of a gel rather than a runny liquid. Though the use of the word jelly through me as in UK thats what we call jello. My Opinion On The Photo Like it!', '2003-06-17 12:49:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(327836, 23717, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Even with your description, I''m confused.  Is the blob an entire bed sheet?  Or is this blob actually pretty small and made up of only the gel?  If so, what in the heck is that string?  This looks like some kind of freaky tampon.<br/>I think I would have focused mainly on the blob.  The background isn''t appealing and does not add to the ''liquid'' in any way.  It''s hard to tell even what this is, if it is liquid, and what is going on.<br/>The focus seems a bit soft.  Either that, or the texture is one that really looks off.  I would have gotten right up close and focussed mainly on the reflections <br/>The little red lights really aren''t very noticable at this distance.  You can''t really tell what they are.  Looks like dirt in the gel or something.<br/>The color of the blob is nice, I like that there are bright blue spots and dark blue spots.<br/>Overall not really much to look at that interests me.  I do think it would have been much more effective if it were closer up.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*Even with your description, I''m confused.  Is the blob an entire bed sheet?  Or is this blob actually pretty small and made up of only the gel?  If so, what in the heck is that string?  This looks like some kind of freaky tampon.I think I would have focused mainly on the blob.  The background isn''t appealing and does not add to the ''liquid'' in any way.  It''s hard to tell even what this is, if it is liquid, and what is going on.The focus seems a bit soft.  Either that, or the texture is one that really looks off.  I would have gotten right up close and focussed mainly on the reflections The little red lights really aren''t very noticable at this distance.  You can''t really tell what they are.  Looks like dirt in the gel or something.The color of the blob is nice, I like that there are bright blue spots and dark blue spots.Overall not really much to look at that interests me.  I do think it would have been much more effective if it were closer up.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 13:53:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(327842, 23814, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Definately gotta straighten this up a bit.  I think that the left tilt hurts this shot a lot in my opinion.<br/>The lighting is really good.  I like how it''s lit from the back.  I do think that the glass being shaped differently helps this shot as well.  A plain straight glass just wouldn''t look as good.<br/>The colors are really good, and I think that because of the lighting, it really helps to bring out the colors in the tea.<br/>The focus looks quite soft on the upper part of the glass, but looks much clearer on the very bottom (base) of the glass.  I think I would prefer this had the upper part been in focus as well.<br/>Overall, interesting abstract looking shot.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*Definately gotta straighten this up a bit.  I think that the left tilt hurts this shot a lot in my opinion.The lighting is really good.  I like how it''s lit from the back.  I do think that the glass being shaped differently helps this shot as well.  A plain straight glass just wouldn''t look as good.The colors are really good, and I think that because of the lighting, it really helps to bring out the colors in the tea.The focus looks quite soft on the upper part of the glass, but looks much clearer on the very bottom (base) of the glass.  I think I would prefer this had the upper part been in focus as well.Overall, interesting abstract looking shot.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 13:59:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(327902, 23383, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC!<br/> <br/>Well well well, what do we have here... from solid to liquid indeed! Hehehehehe. Humor always scores big with me. <br/> <br/>Color, focus and lighting are ok, nothing to say there. I might have cropped it differently and get rid of that right-hand post. Again this is very subjective.<br/> <br/>Of course being a snapshot, you got flamed by the DPCers (me included!! :-P). But for a snapshot, it works well.<br/> <br/>I personnaly don''t think that it truly meets the challenge cuz without your title there isn''t any refference to liquid (uless you look at the rain but t hat''s water and doesn''t count).<br/> <br/>Yanik</td>', 'Greetings from the CC! Well well well, what do we have here... from solid to liquid indeed! Hehehehehe. Humor always scores big with me.  Color, focus and lighting are ok, nothing to say there. I might have cropped it differently and get rid of that right-hand post. Again this is very subjective. Of course being a snapshot, you got flamed by the DPCers (me included!! :-P). But for a snapshot, it works well. I personnaly don''t think that it truly meets the challenge cuz without your title there isn''t any refference to liquid (uless you look at the rain but t hat''s water and doesn''t count). Yanik', '2003-06-17 15:04:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(327923, 23813, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Focus does seem a bit soft on the glass, the noise maker, and the confetti.  It''s really hard to tell how it is on the bubbles cause of their textures.<br/>The angle and framing/cropping are really good. I like how you have included the noise maker, and the confetti, while dark, do add a bit of interest to the shot.<br/>You got a bright blue pixel to the right of the glass.  I''m gonna have to mark you down 4 points for that. lol (just a joke if you follow the forums) Not a big deal.<br/>The lighting on the left of the large part of the glass is a bit harsh.  I do however like it on the bubbles.  I like the little rainbows in the bubbles.<br/>I would definately like to see sharper focus.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*Focus does seem a bit soft on the glass, the noise maker, and the confetti.  It''s really hard to tell how it is on the bubbles cause of their textures.The angle and framing/cropping are really good. I like how you have included the noise maker, and the confetti, while dark, do add a bit of interest to the shot.You got a bright blue pixel to the right of the glass.  I''m gonna have to mark you down 4 points for that. lol (just a joke if you follow the forums) Not a big deal.The lighting on the left of the large part of the glass is a bit harsh.  I do however like it on the bubbles.  I like the little rainbows in the bubbles.I would definately like to see sharper focus.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 15:17:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(327977, 23198, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>First thing I notice is that the focus seems really soft here.  Obviously a low lighting shot, and I think that is making the focus a bit soft here.  Better to set the camera down on something, and use the timer if your camera has one.  That way, you can avoid any camera shake at all.  Could help save the focus that way.<br/>Also, while there is a CAN of liquid in the shot, we really can''t see the actual liquid itself.<br/>I do like how you have the flames in the glass.  I tink that''s a really cool effect.  Makes for an interesting shot for sure.  it''s a little bit too bright in the center of the flames, but I think it doesn''t harm the photo much if at all.<br/>The angle and framing/cropping are ok.  I disagree with the comment about removing the can from the photo.  Without that can, the shot really doesn''t fit the challenge at all.  Even with the can, it''s a bit of a stretch.  Definately keep the can, but do put it on a solid background.  I think that had it been all black, without the light colored area, the flames could have stood out on a total black background nicely.  Especially if it were showing through the glass a bit.<br/>Overall, just needs a bit of sharpness.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*First thing I notice is that the focus seems really soft here.  Obviously a low lighting shot, and I think that is making the focus a bit soft here.  Better to set the camera down on something, and use the timer if your camera has one.  That way, you can avoid any camera shake at all.  Could help save the focus that way.Also, while there is a CAN of liquid in the shot, we really can''t see the actual liquid itself.I do like how you have the flames in the glass.  I tink that''s a really cool effect.  Makes for an interesting shot for sure.  it''s a little bit too bright in the center of the flames, but I think it doesn''t harm the photo much if at all.The angle and framing/cropping are ok.  I disagree with the comment about removing the can from the photo.  Without that can, the shot really doesn''t fit the challenge at all.  Even with the can, it''s a bit of a stretch.  Definately keep the can, but do put it on a solid background.  I think that had it been all black, without the light colored area, the flames could have stood out on a total black background nicely.  Especially if it were showing through the glass a bit.Overall, just needs a bit of sharpness.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 16:20:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(328187, 23559, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>The very first thing I notice here is that the focus is really soft.  I''m actually not really seeing a sharp point of focus.<br/>The lighting hurts mostly, but I do like the little star reflections inside the glass.  I don''t like the bottom side of the glass because it''s really bright, and the glare is white and distracting.  Usually when I use flash, the focus turns out to be a bit nicer, but it doesn''t seem to be the case in your situation.<br/>The background is good.  Nothing distracting in the background.  I think though that the glare near the bottom ON the background is a bit distracting, but that isn''t the background itself.<br/>Definately liquid.  Meets the challenge just fine.  I wonder if a different angle would have helped to reduce the glare.  For the look of the photo, I think the angle is good though.  Also, a little trick I use with my camera is i put a white plastic cup over the flash, and it seems to help a little on glares.  <br/>Overall, an interesting idea.  With better focus and lighting, I think that this would be something neat.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*The very first thing I notice here is that the focus is really soft.  I''m actually not really seeing a sharp point of focus.The lighting hurts mostly, but I do like the little star reflections inside the glass.  I don''t like the bottom side of the glass because it''s really bright, and the glare is white and distracting.  Usually when I use flash, the focus turns out to be a bit nicer, but it doesn''t seem to be the case in your situation.The background is good.  Nothing distracting in the background.  I think though that the glare near the bottom ON the background is a bit distracting, but that isn''t the background itself.Definately liquid.  Meets the challenge just fine.  I wonder if a different angle would have helped to reduce the glare.  For the look of the photo, I think the angle is good though.  Also, a little trick I use with my camera is i put a white plastic cup over the flash, and it seems to help a little on glares.  Overall, an interesting idea.  With better focus and lighting, I think that this would be something neat.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 21:13:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(328192, 23396, 7743, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/><b>General: </b> Focus is sharp and the contrast is good.<br/> <br/><b>Composition: </b>  I think what would probably have helped with composition would be to include the Guinness tap in the background but blurred.<br/> <br/><b>Exposure: </b> I can''t see anything wrong with the exposure.  There are no blown out areas, nor are there any significant dark areas with no detail.<br/> <br/><b>Impact: </b> With the composition you chose I don''t feel that the image has much impact.  A different vantage point or as mentioned above a more interesting background would probably help with this.<br/> <br/>At the very least, I hope you enjoyed the Guinness after you finished shooting it.<br/> <br/>Colette</td>', '*Critique Club* General:  Focus is sharp and the contrast is good. Composition:   I think what would probably have helped with composition would be to include the Guinness tap in the background but blurred. Exposure:  I can''t see anything wrong with the exposure.  There are no blown out areas, nor are there any significant dark areas with no detail. Impact:  With the composition you chose I don''t feel that the image has much impact.  A different vantage point or as mentioned above a more interesting background would probably help with this. At the very least, I hope you enjoyed the Guinness after you finished shooting it. Colette', '2003-06-17 21:33:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(328210, 23788, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>The challenge called for a liquid NOT water.  This looks like water to me, but I have been known to be wrong before.<br/>This is a really really nice photograph.  I like the whole scene as a whole.  I like the steam, the colors and the road in the background.<br/>There are a few things though that I see that as indivudual aspects could have been improved upon.<br/>It''s true that the road does lead OUT of the photo, and in this case only suceeds in creating a distraction.  But, I still like it. lol<br/>The far ''horizon'' where the dirt meets the darker background LOOKS tilted, but it isn''t.  I''m not really sure if it''s the road that creates the illusion that it''s tilted, but something is.<br/>Other than that, I think your focus and clarity are really great, and I could actually see this in the magazine cover challenge.  I think it would work better in that challenge than the liquid without water challenge.<br/>Looks like a very nice place to visit.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*The challenge called for a liquid NOT water.  This looks like water to me, but I have been known to be wrong before.This is a really really nice photograph.  I like the whole scene as a whole.  I like the steam, the colors and the road in the background.There are a few things though that I see that as indivudual aspects could have been improved upon.It''s true that the road does lead OUT of the photo, and in this case only suceeds in creating a distraction.  But, I still like it. lolThe far ''horizon'' where the dirt meets the darker background LOOKS tilted, but it isn''t.  I''m not really sure if it''s the road that creates the illusion that it''s tilted, but something is.Other than that, I think your focus and clarity are really great, and I could actually see this in the magazine cover challenge.  I think it would work better in that challenge than the liquid without water challenge.Looks like a very nice place to visit.~Heather~', '2003-06-17 21:55:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(328215, 23771, 7743, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/><b>First impression: </b> Do you have an underwater (...undermilk) camera?  Great angle on this shot.  <br/> <br/><b>Composition: </b> I get the feeling that this was taken looking directly up at the bubbles.  The only distraction is the small bit of white in the bottom left.  If you crop that out on the vertical then the effect is multiplied.<br/> <br/><b>Exposure: </b> The image seems just slightly dark though I think this was probably on purpose so the milk was not so obvious.<br/> <br/><b>Impact: </b> This image has a lot of impact giving the impression of being underwater.  More impact I think would be achieve if the small portion of visible milk on the left were cropped out.<br/> <br/>Well done.  Keep experimenting, no matter how messy things get.<br/> <br/>Colette</td>', '*Critique Club* First impression:  Do you have an underwater (...undermilk) camera?  Great angle on this shot.   Composition:  I get the feeling that this was taken looking directly up at the bubbles.  The only distraction is the small bit of white in the bottom left.  If you crop that out on the vertical then the effect is multiplied. Exposure:  The image seems just slightly dark though I think this was probably on purpose so the milk was not so obvious. Impact:  This image has a lot of impact giving the impression of being underwater.  More impact I think would be achieve if the small portion of visible milk on the left were cropped out. Well done.  Keep experimenting, no matter how messy things get. Colette', '2003-06-17 21:57:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(328235, 23375, 656, '<td valign="top">CC:  Not sure I really see the liquid in this shot, but that aside.  The flag could be a good subject.  I think to enhance this you need to pay a bit more attention to the background, which is cluttered and a similar colour to the flag. <br/> <br/>As a result the foreground subject merges with the background jumble and makes for a less than clear overall impression/ message.  You''ve accentuated this by having the lighter/ whiter part of the flag against the rather blown out sky, again merging and losing the foreground focus.<br/> <br/>Hope this is useful.</td>', 'CC:  Not sure I really see the liquid in this shot, but that aside.  The flag could be a good subject.  I think to enhance this you need to pay a bit more attention to the background, which is cluttered and a similar colour to the flag.  As a result the foreground subject merges with the background jumble and makes for a less than clear overall impression/ message.  You''ve accentuated this by having the lighter/ whiter part of the flag against the rather blown out sky, again merging and losing the foreground focus. Hope this is useful.', '2003-06-17 22:13:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(328237, 23497, 656, '<td valign="top">Critique club:<br/> <br/>Initial reactions:  Your final score is far too low for such an interesting shot.<br/> <br/>Your entered picture is too small - - it takes up less than 1/10th of my total screen - you might do better in future if you try to use the full 640px size that you are allowed.<br/> <br/>Nice colours, and interesting shapes, with perhaps the distortion in the upper right being a bit out of place.  I like the fairly agressive composition with the yellow dot in the corner and the red exploding around it.  The purple/ pink complements the other colours well too.  I think the fact that it is blurry actually helps too to make it even more abstract.  Finally something different, but these types of shots often do not go down well with the general voting populace.<br/> <br/>Also as a general point, while abstracts are a great form of photography, they are going to suffer in a challenge were a clearly defined subject is mentioned - this might have fared better in some of the colour challenges<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-06-17 21:20:40.</i></td>', 'Critique club: Initial reactions:  Your final score is far too low for such an interesting shot. Your entered picture is too small - - it takes up less than 1/10th of my total screen - you might do better in future if you try to use the full 640px size that you are allowed. Nice colours, and interesting shapes, with perhaps the distortion in the upper right being a bit out of place.  I like the fairly agressive composition with the yellow dot in the corner and the red exploding around it.  The purple/ pink complements the other colours well too.  I think the fact that it is blurry actually helps too to make it even more abstract.  Finally something different, but these types of shots often do not go down well with the general voting populace. Also as a general point, while abstracts are a great form of photography, they are going to suffer in a challenge were a clearly defined subject is mentioned - this might have fared better in some of the colour challenges', '2003-06-17 22:18:01.000000', '2003-06-17 22:20:40.000000', 0),
(328249, 23559, 6886, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the Critiques.</td>', 'Thanks for all the Critiques.', '2003-06-17 22:52:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(328288, 23720, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Very nice image, definately meets the challenge, your focus is fantastic on this image.<br/> <br/>The only thing I see that probably hurt you is the shadow and glare both going on in the same area just above the handle of the mug.  It distracts from the entire image although it would also be hard to correct.<br/> <br/>Very well done.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* Very nice image, definately meets the challenge, your focus is fantastic on this image. The only thing I see that probably hurt you is the shadow and glare both going on in the same area just above the handle of the mug.  It distracts from the entire image although it would also be hard to correct. Very well done. Anna', '2003-06-18 00:34:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(328302, 23712, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Interesting take for the liquid challenge definately meets the challenge.  The focus is good.  The composition is ok but might improve by being cropped so the egg isn''t in the center.  The one thing I don''t like here is the color of the egg itself the shell is to creamy colored and the yolk just isn''t as yellow as it should be which could possibly just be the lighting was to dark to bring out the true colors of the egg.<br/> <br/>Very nice take for the liquid challenge keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* Interesting take for the liquid challenge definately meets the challenge.  The focus is good.  The composition is ok but might improve by being cropped so the egg isn''t in the center.  The one thing I don''t like here is the color of the egg itself the shell is to creamy colored and the yolk just isn''t as yellow as it should be which could possibly just be the lighting was to dark to bring out the true colors of the egg. Very nice take for the liquid challenge keep up the good work. Anna', '2003-06-18 00:47:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(328305, 23795, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>Only got a few minutes, but I figured a short comment is better than no comment.<br/>The shadow in the back distracts from the image in my opinion.  I do like the refreshingness of this shot.  I think that the close up really makes this for me.<br/>Your focus and clarity of the ice is really good.  I like the detail in the ice actually.  You captured the texture very well.<br/>I also like the bubbles from pouring it in.<br/>Something is missing though.  I''m not really sure what.  It''s just not holding my attention like I want it to.  Maybe it''s how it gets dark in the back and than leads to the shadows on the wall?<br/>Overall decently done, just not my cup of....whatever it is. lol<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*Only got a few minutes, but I figured a short comment is better than no comment.The shadow in the back distracts from the image in my opinion.  I do like the refreshingness of this shot.  I think that the close up really makes this for me.Your focus and clarity of the ice is really good.  I like the detail in the ice actually.  You captured the texture very well.I also like the bubbles from pouring it in.Something is missing though.  I''m not really sure what.  It''s just not holding my attention like I want it to.  Maybe it''s how it gets dark in the back and than leads to the shadows on the wall?Overall decently done, just not my cup of....whatever it is. lol~Heather~', '2003-06-18 00:51:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(328307, 23418, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Eyecatching colors.  Good focus and nice use of dof.  The bubbles in the brandy seem be take away from the overall image rather than add to it.  they make the brandy appear to be out of focus, which really isn''t the case.  <br/> <br/>Again it is an eye catcher, and does fit the challenge well.  Nicely done.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club* Eyecatching colors.  Good focus and nice use of dof.  The bubbles in the brandy seem be take away from the overall image rather than add to it.  they make the brandy appear to be out of focus, which really isn''t the case.   Again it is an eye catcher, and does fit the challenge well.  Nicely done. Anna', '2003-06-18 00:54:07.000000', NULL, 0),
