(462988, 23666, 6999, '<td valign="top">For those intersted, Dr Harold Edgerton, known simply as ''Doc'' to his students at MIT, was the pioneer of this trick - indeed his ''Milk Drop Coronet'' is perhaps his most famous image, and prints of it sell for phenomenal sums. This is perhaps better than his shot - but then the poor guy was shooting in the 1940''s (I think), with his own new invention, the strobe flash: we now, of course, have the good fortune to be able to see our shots straight away, and select the best. Worth checking out his work, if you get the chance.<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'For those intersted, Dr Harold Edgerton, known simply as ''Doc'' to his students at MIT, was the pioneer of this trick - indeed his ''Milk Drop Coronet'' is perhaps his most famous image, and prints of it sell for phenomenal sums. This is perhaps better than his shot - but then the poor guy was shooting in the 1940''s (I think), with his own new invention, the strobe flash: we now, of course, have the good fortune to be able to see our shots straight away, and select the best. Worth checking out his work, if you get the chance. Ed', '2003-10-27 05:44:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(484856, 23215, 12611, '<td valign="top">I love this shot. It is an instinct of all city photographers to shoot straight up a building- and most of the it doesn''t work. THis shot works. Its got big circles, a huge rise, it makes me dizzy and makes my neck hurt, in other words... alright!!!!</td>', 'I love this shot. It is an instinct of all city photographers to shoot straight up a building- and most of the it doesn''t work. THis shot works. Its got big circles, a huge rise, it makes me dizzy and makes my neck hurt, in other words... alright!!!!', '2003-11-13 22:30:29.000000', NULL, 0),
